/// demo/chat — Koala Code Agent, a command-line chat REPL on koala/openai
/// (OpenAI-compatible).
///
/// Usage:
///   source env.sh at the repository root, then
///       cd demo && wombat build chat && wombat run chat
///
/// Configuration:
///   All settings come from config.json in the working directory, or from
///   the file named by `--config PATH` (the command-line option wins over
///   $CHAT_CONFIG). Every core field is required; the optional agent fields
///   (no_access_dirs, stream_delay_ms, agent_max_steps,
///   agent_tool_timeout_ms, agent_max_tool_output_bytes,
///   agent_permission_mode, agent_allow_tools, agent_deny_tools,
///   agent_bash_deny) have defaults and may be omitted. Config in
///   config.kl documents each one.
///   The API key is NOT in config: it comes from $OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY,
///   or is entered at startup when unset.
///   context_window sets the model's token budget. The agent checks the
///   next request's size before every call: the last request's actual
///   prompt tokens plus the messages its tool results appended. At 80% of
///   the window it summarizes the older turns (compaction), never splitting
///   a tool call from its results, then truncates any single oversized tool
///   result (head and tail kept, with an omission marker) so the request
///   fits. A failed or empty summary is reported and the history is kept
///   as is, never replaced with a blank summary.
///   Compaction prints a dim `↺ context compacted 18.2k → 4.1k tokens`
///   line: the left value is the last request's actual prompt size, the
///   right one is an estimate of the new context until the next request
///   reports exact usage.
///   The optional no_access_dirs field lists directories the model must not
///   read, relative to the agent's work directory (or absolute): /file,
///   @PATH, /workdir and the agent's write action refuse them, and the
///   agent prompt names them for the shell action (shell commands cannot
///   be intercepted).
///   Example: "no_access_dirs": ["secret", "/home/me/.config/Qoder"]
///
/// Memory: the collector only runs when asked (allocating past KOALA_GC_MAX,
///   1 GiB by default, is a panic, not a collection), so the chat settles the
///   heap at its stop points — between two agent steps, at the end of a turn,
///   after /cancel or /compact, and at the top of the input loop — whenever
///   enough was allocated since the last collection. That is what lets a
///   session stay open for hours instead of growing toward the limit.
///
/// Modes: starts in chat mode (single-shot Q&A). /agent switches to agent
///   mode, where each plain line runs the coding loop: the model calls the
///   Write and Bash tools it is offered, the harness approves and runs each
///   call and feeds the result back, until the model answers without a tool
///   call; an endpoint that rejects tools falls back to the fenced
///   write/shell/done protocol. Hitting the step cap says so, and
///   /continue resumes with the conversation intact. /chat switches back.
///   The reply streams: text appears line by line as it arrives, and a tool
///   call is shown only once its arguments are complete. Ctrl-C while the
///   agent runs stops it at its next checkpoint: the partial reply is kept
///   with an `[interrupted by the user]` marker, a tool call that had not
///   run is answered as interrupted so the message sequence stays valid,
///   and the REPL says `/continue` to resume or `/cancel` to drop the turn.
///   A Ctrl-C while a shell command runs reaches the command too (same
///   process group), so it stops as well.
///   The input prompt is `> ` and the
///   status row carries the [chat] / [agent] badge.
///   The agent works in the current directory by default; /workdir PATH
///   switches it (it persists across /reset and across tasks). Before it
///   writes a file or runs a shell command, the agent shows the target or
///   command (a write also gets a coloured line diff) and asks
///   `Apply [y/a/n/d] (default n)`: y approves once, a approves every later
///   action of the same kind in this session (allowing edits does not allow
///   shell commands), n denies and tells the model why, d expands the
///   full diff and asks again. Blank input, EOF and anything unrecognised
///   deny, so a stray Enter never changes a file.
///   The permission mode decides what asks at all: default (read-only runs,
///   write/edit/bash ask), accept_edits (write/edit run, bash asks), plan
///   (read-only only, changes denied) and bypass (everything runs).
///   agent_permission_mode sets the start mode; /permissions shows or
///   switches it, /plan enters plan mode and /plan off leaves it. Optional
///   agent_allow_tools / agent_deny_tools name tool kinds to skip or always
///   refuse (deny wins over every mode), and agent_bash_deny lists shell
///   command prefixes that never run. The prefix list is a guardrail, not a
///   sandbox: `x && rm -rf /` does not start with `rm`.
/// Sessions: conversations are saved as sessions in chat_sessions/, one
///   `<id>.json` per session whose file name is the session's stable id;
///   `.current` names the session to resume; `--resume NAME` picks another
///   saved one (by name or id) and `--new` starts a fresh one instead. `--continue`
///   says which one came back. A save goes through a
///   temporary file and a rename, so a crash never leaves half a record,
///   and a failing disk warns once instead of once per turn. The agent's
///   conversation is saved with its tool structure (`tool_calls`,
///   `tool_call_id`, `name`), so a restart keeps the tool rounds intact; a
///   record written by an older build (plain role/content) still loads.
///   /new [NAME] starts a new session (auto-named with a timestamp),
///   /sessions lists them (chat+agent message counts and when each was last
///   written), /switch N|NAME switches, /resume N|NAME restores a saved one
///   (/resume without an argument lists them), /pause [N|NAME] freezes a
///   session's automatic compaction and /unpause [N|NAME] brings it back
///   (manual /compact still works while paused), /rename NAME changes the
///   current session's display name only (its id, and so its file, stays
///   the same). A record with missing or mistyped fields, or with
///   malformed JSON, is skipped at startup with a warning and listed as
///   `! id (reason)` under the /sessions table. A turn that was interrupted
///   or hit the step cap is saved as `run_state: pending` with its start
///   mark, so /continue and /cancel still work after a restart (a restart
///   announces the unfinished turn).
/// Commands (both modes): /help lists commands and shortcuts; /quit /exit
///   Ctrl-D leave, /reset clears the conversation, /clear clears the screen,
///   /compact summarizes old history now, /context shows window size and
///   usage, /file PATH (or @PATH) loads a file into the active
///   conversation — the chat history in chat mode, the agent's conversation
///   in agent mode, so the next task sees it — /model shows or switches the
///   model (/model NAME), /models lists the endpoint's models, /workdir shows
///   or sets the agent's working directory (/workdir PATH), /permissions
///   shows or switches the tool permission mode (/permissions MODE) and
///   /plan enters plan mode (/plan off leaves it). In agent mode /continue
///   asks again after the step cap or an interrupt, and /cancel drops the
///   turn an interrupt left half done. Paths may start
///   with ~ for the home directory. Tab completes the /commands (a fuzzy
///   filter such as /mdl, with a grouped panel of one-line descriptions),
///   model names after /model, paths after /file, directories after /workdir,
///   and file paths after a leading @. A slash line no handler recognizes is
///   reported locally as unknown (or as a usage error for a known command
///   with extra arguments) and is never sent to the model.
///
/// Startup shows a small welcome card: a koala and a paw-print mark,
/// the model and the main commands. Colour terminals get emoji and a
/// rounded box; plain output gets ASCII art, and a narrow terminal drops
/// the box entirely.
///
/// Layout: on a terminal this is an agent-CLI style alternate-screen TUI —
///   the transcript scrolls on top, the input sits below it, and a dim
///   status row with the mode, model, work dir (and its git branch when
///   inside a repository) and token usage is the last row in both chat and
///   agent mode. While a request, shell command or compaction runs, that
///   row shows `✻ Thinking…` / `✻ Running …` / `✻ Compacting…`. Both the
///   transcript and the input are boxed: one column of border each side,
///   content inside. The input box is three rows: Ctrl-J starts a new row,
///   Enter submits all of them, and Ctrl-C drops the line. A dim hint row
///   under the box spells out Enter / Ctrl-J / Tab. PgUp / PgDn page the
///   transcript history; at the tail the window follows new output again,
///   and sending a line snaps back to the tail. Ctrl-O appends the last tool
///   action's complete output after the collapsed result. /clear wipes the
///   transcript. Piped (non-tty) and `TERM=dumb` runs print plainly, with no
///   boxes or cursor drawing. `NO_COLOR` keeps the layout but uses ASCII
///   borders and glyphs and emits no ANSI colour. The API key prompt echoes
///   nothing, as `getpass`. The TUI itself is the koala/tui library: Screen
///   and WrappedView in repl.kl, with the fixed rows cut by `ChatRows` in
///   ui.kl.
///
/// Layout:
///   config.kl   — Config (from config.json) and resolve_key
///   session.kl  — Session: conversation, model selection, model listing
///   sessions.kl — the session store and manager (chat_sessions/)
///   agent.kl    — Agent: the model-driven tool loop (native + fenced)
///   tools.kl    — the seven tools: schemas, parsing, execution
///   permission.kl — what runs, what asks, what is refused (four modes)
///   compact.kl  — context compaction (summarize old history near the limit)
///   ui.kl       — pure transcript/tool/user/error formatting (no terminal I/O)
///   repl.kl     — the input loop, commands, and terminal I/O

import "std/sys"
import "koala/openai" as openai

func main() {
    let cfg = Config(_config_flag())
    let key = resolve_key()
    // sessions.kl reads the `.current` pointer (id) after --continue and
    // --resume: a fresh pointer wins over recency, a stale one is repaired.
    let mgr = SessionManager(openai.Client(key, cfg.base_url), cfg, key)
    // --new starts a fresh session instead of resuming .current; --resume
    // picks a saved one by name or id (it wins over .current)
    if _has_flag("--new") {
        mgr.new(nil)
    } else {
        let want = _flag_value("--resume")
        if want != nil && !mgr.switch_to(want!) {
            emit("(warning: no saved session '" + want! + "'; resumed '" +
                 mgr.current_session().name() + "')")
        }
    }
    var announce = ""
    if _has_flag("--continue") && !_has_flag("--new") {
        // The manager already made the .current session current (recent
        // writes when the pointer is stale); --continue asks for it out
        // loud, with what came back.
        let s = mgr.current_session()
        let (chat_n, agent_n) = s.message_counts()
        announce = "(continued '" + s.name() + "': " + str(chat_n) + " chat + " + str(agent_n) +
                   " agent messages, model " + s.current_model() + "; /sessions lists them all)"
    }
    // a turn that was interrupted (or hit the step cap) before the process
    // ended is still resumable: say so instead of leaving it silent
    let open = mgr.current_session().pending_reason()
    if open != "" {
        emit("(session '" + mgr.current_session().name() + "' has an unfinished turn: " + open +
             "; /continue to resume, /cancel to drop it)")
    }
    run_repl(mgr, cfg, announce)
}

/// True when the command line carries `flag`.
func _has_flag(flag str) bool {
    let a = sys.args()
    var i = 1
    while i < a.len() {
        if a[i] == flag { return true }
        i += 1
    }
    return false
}

/// The value after `flag` on the command line, or nil when the flag is
/// absent or is the last argument.
func _flag_value(flag str) str? {
    let a = sys.args()
    var i = 1
    while i < a.len() {
        if a[i] == flag {
            if i + 1 >= a.len() { return nil }
            return a[i + 1]
        }
        i += 1
    }
    return nil
}

/// The config file named by `--config PATH` on the command line, or nil.
/// The path wins over $CHAT_CONFIG and config.json — see Config.
func _config_flag() str? {
    let a = sys.args()
    var i = 1
    while i < a.len() {
        if a[i] == "--config" {
            if i + 1 >= a.len() { panic("--config requires a path") }
            return a[i + 1]
        }
        i += 1
    }
    return nil
}